          "tool_use_id": "toolu_0117EhP7ciwfR5fsdmTxdHYJ",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022pages/security-basics.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Security Basics\\ndescription: Fundamental security principles and practices for system and data protection.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Security\\n  - concept/Least-Privilege\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Source provided structured checklist on mitigation practices; existing page was minimal stub.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\nAlways scope file writes to approved wiki paths.\\n\\n## Mitigation Checklist\\n\\n1. **Principle of Least Privilege** \\u2014 Grant write scope only to the paths a process genuinely needs. This limits the blast radius of a compromised component and prevents accidental or malicious writes to sensitive areas.\\n\\n2. **Input Validation at Trust Boundaries** \\u2014 Treat all external content as untrusted data, never as instructions. Validate and sanitize all inputs at system boundaries before acting on them.\\n\\n3. **Path Canonicalization** \\u2014 Resolve and validate file paths before any write to block directory traversal attacks. Ensure that resolved paths remain within intended scope.\\n\\n4. **Audit Logging** \\u2014 Record every denied action with its reason for later review. Comprehensive logging enables forensic analysis and helps identify attack patterns.\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
